Part 2: Learning SignalR by building Projects

PROJECT 2 

Task Manger: A continuation project from Project 1(Notification Board) as i learn SignalR

Diificulty : Hard 

In Project 1, i built a one-to-many broadcast system: one admin sends, everyone receives. 
That pattern is elegant precisely because it is simple — the server never needs to think about which clients should receive which messages. 
It sends to Clients.All and its job is done.


WHAT CHANGES NOW?
Task Manger adds Many senders, many receivers. 
Background Service: Runs on the background and checks for overdue task that are not marked as complete on our concurrent dictonary
Messages are filtered by project board (Groups). 
Shared application state must stay consistent across all clients. 
Identity matters — who claimed a task? Reconnection must restore full board state
On this project, I also added  a background service that periodically checks for overdue tasks and notifies the relevant board. 


THE SCRENARION PROBLEM
It is six months after i deployed the Meridian Technologies notification board [Project 1 - NOTIFICATION BOARD]. 
The Engineering team has grown to twelve developers across three squads. 
They are using a third-party task tracking tool that has a frustrating limitation: when one developer updates a task's status, no one else knows until they manually refresh. 
In one incident, two developers both claimed the same unassigned task and spent four hours building the same feature in parallel. 
The post-mortem cost-of-mistake was estimated at 32 developer hours.

So they asked me to build an internal replacement — a live collaborative task board. 

The requirements:

Multiple project boards — Each squad has its own board. 
Changes to one board must not appear on others.
Live task creation — When any developer creates a task, it appears on every connected teammate's board instantly.
Live status updates — Moving a task from "To Do" → "In Progress" → "Done" is immediately reflected everywhere.
Task claiming — A developer can "claim" an unassigned task. The moment they do, it shows their name as the assignee on all other screens, and the Claim button disappears (it's already claimed).
Live viewer count per board — Each board shows how many teammates are currently viewing it.
State recovery on reconnect — If a developer's connection drops and reconnects, they receive the full current board state, not a blank page.
Overdue task alerts — A background process checks for overdue tasks and notifies the relevant board's viewers (uses IHubContext).

HOW TO TEST

REQUIREMENTS
.NET 8 SDK
VS CODE

Clone the repository

Use the command "dotnet run" on the terminal
You shou see something like this # Now listening on: http://localhost:5000


Test Scenario 1 — Basic Group Isolation

Open Window A at http://localhost:5000. Enter name "Name 1" and board ID "engineering". Click Join. You should see the setup screen replaced by the empty board. Viewer count shows 1.

Open Window B at http://localhost:5000. Enter name "Name 2" and board ID "engineering". Click Join. 
Watch Window A — you should see a toast: "A teammate joined the board." 
Viewer count on both windows shows 2.

Open Window C at http://localhost:5000. Enter name "Name 3" and board ID "devops" (different board). 
Click Join. Watch Window A and B — they should NOT show a "teammate joined" toast. 
Name 3 is on a different board in a different group. Windows A and B still show viewer count 2.

This confirms Groups work correctly — Name 4 joining DevOps doesn't affect the Engineering board watchers at all.
